          {
            "abbreviated": false,
            "abstract": "A Lean 4 / Mathlib formalization of three classical results on edge-disjoint trees and forests in finite simple graphs, none of which is in Mathlib. (1) The Nash-Williams\u2013Tutte tree packing theorem: a graph has k edge-disjoint spanning trees if and only if every partition of its vertices into m parts has at least k(m \u2212 1) edges between different parts. (2) Nash-Williams' arboricity theorem: the edges can be covered by k forests if and only if every nonempty vertex set S spans at most k(|S| \u2212 1) edges. (3) The corollary that every 2k-edge-connected graph has k edge-disjoint spanning trees. Mathlib has no matroid union theorem and no graphic matroid; the proofs are direct edge-exchange arguments.",
            "authors": [
              {
                "name": "Juan Pablo Traverso Gianini"
              }
            ],
            "classification": {
              "arxiv": [
                "math.CO"
              ],
              "msc2020": [
